  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Gusty winds through this afternoon. Frost in the \nmountains tomorrow morning. \n\nIn the wake of a departing cold front, a cooler and drier airmass \nwill settle over the region. Northwesterly flow aloft on the \nbackside of the 500mb trough will allow for much cooler air to \ncontinue to flow into the region. Gusty north to northwesterly \nwinds will continue into the evening, between 15 to 25 mph for \nmost, and up to 35 mph along the higher ridgelines. Winds along \nthe Blue Ridge and the Piedmont may stay slightly breezy into \nthe early morning hours of Sunday, which could keep the \natmosphere mixed well enough to keep temperatures a few degrees \nwarmer than the more sheltered mountain valleys in the west. \nWith clear skies overhead, temperatures are expected to fall \ninto the upper 20s to low 30s in the mountains, and mid to upper\n30s in the Piedmont. A Freeze Warning and Frost Advisory remain\nin effect for the mountain counties from late tonight through \nSunday morning. Efforts should be made today to protect \nsensitive vegetation by covering it or moving it indoors. \nSunday night into Monday morning will be cold as well, so still \nplan to keep vegetation covered or inside for another night, and\nto wear a heavier coat or an extra layer or two while waiting \nat the bus stop. \n\nSurface high pressure will keep the weather dry for the next \nworkweek, with broad 500mb troughing remaining over the eastern \nthird of the US through the end of the week. After Monday, \ntemperatures start to moderate towards normal, which means highs in \nthe 60s and 70s, and lows in the 30s and 40s.",
